{"name":"Pick Your Brain","hex":"#f1919a","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"pick","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A tool used for digging; a pickaxe.","An anchor."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To grasp and pull with the fingers or fingernails.","To harvest a fruit or vegetable for consumption by removing it from the plant to which it is attached; to harvest an entire plant by removing it from the ground."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/pick"},{"term":"your","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Determiner","definitions":["Belonging to you; of you; related to you (singular; one owner).","Belonging to you; of you; related to you (plural; more owners)."]},{"part":"Contraction","definitions":["Misspelling of you're."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/your"},{"term":"brain","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The control center of the central nervous system of an animal located in the skull, consisting of the cerebrum, cerebellum, and brainstem, which is responsible for perception, cognition, attention, memory, emotion, and action.","A part of the brain, especially associated with particular mental functions, abilities, etc."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To dash out the brains of; to kill by smashing the skull.","To strike (someone) on the head."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/brain"}],"entries":[{"title":"Pick Your Brain","blocks":[{"text":"Pick Your Brain is an American syndicated game show that featured children as contestants. The series ran on weekends for one year, debuting on September 18, 1993, and running until May 21, 1994. Pick Your Brain was created by Marc Summers, who also served as its host and executive producer. He was assisted by a giant robot named 2-XL, based on the Tiger Electronics toy of the same name and voiced by Greg Berg."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pick_Your_Brain","wikidata":"Q7190721"},{"title":"You","blocks":[{"text":"In Modern English, the word \"you\" is the second-person pronoun. It is grammatically plural, and was historically used only for the dative case, but in most modern dialects is used for all cases and numbers."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/You","wikidata":"Q1165897"},{"title":"Brain","blocks":[{"text":"The brain is an organ that serves as the center of the nervous system in all vertebrate and most invertebrate animals. It consists of nervous tissue and is typically located in the head (cephalization), usually near organs for special senses such as vision, hearing, and olfaction. Being the most specialized organ, it is responsible for receiving information from the sensory nervous system, processing that information and the coordination of motor control and the endocrine system."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/14/Chimp_Brain_in_a_jar.jpg/330px-Chimp_Brain_in_a_jar.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brain","wikidata":"Q1073"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cc/Brain_model_-_Insula.jpg/500px-Brain_model_-_Insula.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cc/Brain_model_-_Insula.jpg/500px-Brain_model_-_Insula.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABrain_model_-_Insula.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/81/Section_through_olfactory_bulb_16_days_old_rat_brain.jpg/500px-Section_through_olfactory_bulb_16_days_old_rat_brain.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/81/Section_through_olfactory_bulb_16_days_old_rat_brain.jpg/500px-Section_through_olfactory_bulb_16_days_old_rat_brain.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":" Cross section of the olfactory bulb of a rat, stained in two different ways at the same time: one stain shows neuronal cell bodies, the other shows receptors for the neurotransmitter GABA.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASection_through_olfactory_bulb_16_days_old_rat_brain.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/30/Chemical_synapse_schema_cropped.jpg/500px-Chemical_synapse_schema_cropped.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/30/Chemical_synapse_schema_cropped.jpg/500px-Chemical_synapse_schema_cropped.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Neurons generate electrical signals that travel along their axons. When an electrical impulse reaches a junction called a synapse, it causes a neurotransmitter to be released, which binds to receptors on other cells and thereby alters their electrical activity.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AChemical_synapse_schema_cropped.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4c/Drosophila_melanogaster_-_side_%28aka%29.jpg/500px-Drosophila_melanogaster_-_side_%28aka%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4c/Drosophila_melanogaster_-_side_%28aka%29.jpg/500px-Drosophila_melanogaster_-_side_%28aka%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Fruit flies (Drosophila) have been extensively studied to gain insight into the role of genes in brain development.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ADrosophila_melanogaster_-_side_(aka).jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b4/Shark_brain.png/330px-Shark_brain.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https